00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.000
By the way, has a Mr. Hobart Hume III been asking for me?

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:11.290
Mr. Hobart Hume III, owner of the Snarleyville Toxic Chemical Company, one of the truly great

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:18.680
chemical companies from here to Snarleyville, and he is president of the Nickel Air Club.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.970
He's gonna be coming by the station today, and if I can impress him, he's gonna let me join.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:30.000
So what's all that stuff for, Schemer? Yeah. Well, these are about a few modern

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.440
for Mr. Hume's elegant snacking enjoyment.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.640
Ta-da!

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.120
Here, this is some toffee my mommy made

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.590
and my personal favorite, the cake.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.030
Hey, get your hands off of there!

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.620
I mean, remove your hands at once, young man.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.570
Now, afterwards, if there's anything left,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.970
maybe you can share some of this with me.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.000
I always eat that.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.340
the head first. And, uh, I'm sure Mr. Hume does, too.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.230
How do you know Mr. Hume likes to eat the head first?

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:12.210
Because Mr. Hume and I are the same kind of people, that's why. Now get out of my way,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:18.660
I gotta practice. Ah, Mr. and Mrs. Hume. Charmed, I'm sure.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:25.720
Now, by the way, would you care to partake in some of Mama's homemade toffee?

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.000
What's that? Prefer me to have some first?

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:41.890
a ta-ta of you.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:55.100
- Like Schemer's toffee.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.650
- Hume the third has to come to check out the station.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.500
- Yeah.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.000
- What's that got to do with Schemer being

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.870
Maybe Hobart wants to see if he can hold club meetings here.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.960
Did I hear someone mention a club?

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.450
Mr. Conductor, Schema's going to join the Nicolair Club.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.410
Well, clubs can be fun.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.230
I belong to two myself.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.080
One is the Messy Club.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.050
Once a month, the Messy Club meets and makes a big mess of things.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.050
My house, my backyard, you name it.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.280
I do that all the time.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.390
And I'm not even in a club.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.000
But then, who has to clean it up?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.230
Who else?

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:33.250
The Clean Club.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.170
I'm a member of that one, too.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.000
They're both very exclusive,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.950
which means that not just anybody can join.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.350
What do you have to do to become a member?

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.470
You have to be me.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.360
I'm the only member of each one.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.110
In fact, I'm off to a meeting

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.040
of the Messy Club right now.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.930
I can't wait to mess things up.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.330
I'll tell you how it goes.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.410
I'm late.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.780
I hope I don't start without me.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.840
Children!

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:05.710
I have been to my tailor, picked up my tux, and we are moving uptown.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:10.170
Yeah, if Schemer gets in the Nickel Air Club, we'll go too.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.730
Can't you just see this old jukebox in their fancy schmancy lounge?

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:21.550
Important people gathering all around with lots of nickels.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.530
And Schemer actually showing some class for once.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:30.000
I mean, can you dig it?

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.730
Oh, can you dig it?

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.060
Could someone help me, please?

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.880
Ah, Mr. Hume, I presume?

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:42.960
And you are?

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.540
Yes, I am...

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:53.040
At your side, in your pocket, and under your thumb.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.530
Ah, the candidate.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.000
Let me show you around the station, shall I, please?

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:08.420
Uh, ah, yes, this, uh, this desk, it's, uh, rather amusing, isn't it?

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.440
Please, heavens.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.440
Oh, yes.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.430
Ah, miss, would you kindly tell the station manager that I'm here?

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.190
I am the station manager.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.210
I do not wish to speak to this girl, I wish to speak to the station manager himself.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:22.120
Herself.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.850
I'm the station manager, Stacy Jones.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:30.000
Ah, uh, very well. Hobart Hume III.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.460
- Jones, yes, I know that name.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.260
- Well, it's rather common.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.030
- Yes, my dear, I'm afraid it is.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.200
- Billy Two Feathers.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.160
- Oh, really?

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.020
Translated from the French, du plume?

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.630
- No, from the, um, le Côte à Sous.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:48.950
- Ah, ha, ha, ha.

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.090
Well, good for you, it takes such courage

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.390
to be an Indian in today's world.

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:56.730
- We don't say Indian around Shining Time Station.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.000
We say Native American.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.000
- Ah.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.020
Yes, and a terrific Native American as well.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.880
Schema, this is all rather tedious.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.200
Perhaps you could provide some entertainment,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.100
or perhaps the children could.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:13.580
A song, maybe?

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.060
Yes, they'd love to.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.600
You're kidding, right?

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.200
Oh, Dan, don't be so juvenile, Dan.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.670
So then, old man, old boy,

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:29.920
how does...

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:53.040
Joe Clark sound. You know that one don't you? Of course not. Well, whatever you say. Time. Let's sell it. Come on, Nicholas, here we come.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:11.250
Very amusing indeed how clever to keep children on the hand to entertain. Ah, yes, it is rather clever

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:18.200
And how about that music home dreadful of course? Yes. Yes, of course it was but the

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:22.270
The jukebox oh

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:27.060
Utterly appalling naturally no one at the club would be caught dead operating such a thing

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.000
I mean one isn't a teenager after all this one

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:39.620
No, one isn't a teenager at all, but then again, uh, the arcade, a rather sad little joke, don't you think?

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.180
Schemer doesn't think so, he loves it!

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:43.720
Yeah, he loves it!

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:49.570
Yes, yes, he loves, he loves, he loves, uh, thinking about doing other things with the space.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:55.770
Uh, for example, um, setting up a booth and selling toxic chemicals, hm?

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:59.270
Interesting. We might discuss that at some other time.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:00.000
Yes.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:02.040
We might discuss it a little bit.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:07.480
I don't get it.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.090
I mean, we didn't play that bad.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.030
Maybe somebody wasn't trying.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:14.830
Hmm.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.680
Don't look at me.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.940
If you want to blame somebody, blame that Hobart Hume.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.020
Hey, if Schemer joins that club, Tex, what happens to us?

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:30.000
I suppose they'll just leave us here, Rex.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:45.760
sell us to an antique store and put us up for sale real cheap oh in the basement with

