a sheet over us till the end of time I've been wondering if your

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:06.380
really one of our kind. Oh, I don't mind that you work for a living. We're much more tolerant

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:11.300
of that sort of thing than we used to be. Alas. Well, things were quite different fifty

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:17.270
years ago. Isn't that right, sir? They were indeed. You seem to know your history, Miss

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:23.270
Jones. I bet you're a treasure trove of folksy railroad lore. Why not entertain us with some

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:30.000
railroad stories, Miss Jones? Oh, you don't really want to hear my story, sir. Indulge me,

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.990
tales of working people?

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.030
- Well, I'm quite busy today, sir.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:37.720
- And I bet you tell them every bit as well as a man.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.980
- Perhaps some other time, sir.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:44.000
- Maybe we should go and have this meeting in my workshop.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.200
- Well, that is not fair.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.790
I mean, someone has got to make my visit here more bearable.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:53.930
Folk songs, little children, uncooperative women,

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:57.430
really, schema, what kind of frightful place is this?

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.000
- Well, um, it's, it, uh--

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.670
Billy Two Feathers.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.750
How about a demonstration of your people's wonderful culture?

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:10.930
A rain dance, a war dance, some medicine man mumbo-jumbo. Surprise me.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.270
Oh, I'd love to surprise you.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:14.190
Ahem.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:17.790
Ha, ha, ha, quite a getter he is.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:19.860
Yes, how droll.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.440
Well, perhaps some other time, chief.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.520
Chief?

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:30.000
Er, yes, yes, chief. Chief, er, chief, ha, ha, chief big joke, chief laughing gas.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:34.230
He's only joking. He appears to be obnoxious, but he's only joking.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:38.910
And Stacey here, why, certainly she appears to be sarcastic and snippy, but it's all a...

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:40.370
It's a joke.

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:44.840
And, uh, take the children, please.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:49.040
But, uh, seriously, though, they appear rude, but they're just joking.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:53.920
It's... it's... it's just a joke. It's all just one big joke.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:13:00.000
One... one... horrible, horrible joke. The whole station is just one...

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:06.800
And, well, if you don't like it, then don't blame me, because I don't like it either.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:07.800
Either.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:09.010
Neither.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.290
Neither.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:13.670
Schema, I've had my doubts about you.

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:15.420
Well, haven't we all?

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:17.430
But I think you're beginning to show promise.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.600
I think you may just be our kind.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:25.160
I'm going to return to the club and discuss this with my colleagues.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.450
Then I shall return.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.620
Miss Jones, Chief Two-Feathers' children.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:39.360
So delightful meeting you.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:44.970
And we're delighted as well. Ta-ta.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:48.560
All right!

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.180
Oh, is he something or what?

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.060
Oh, yeah, he's something, all right.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:55.720
I know just what that something is.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:57.200
Class.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.670
Total class with a capital K.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.940
I tell you, though, I have to take some of the credit

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.930
because I, uh, certainly knew how to handle that guy.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:07.920
Schemer.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:10.190
Why would you ever want to be like him?

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:14.590
Your Mr. Hume is the most insulting, arrogant, prejudiced person I have ever met.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.240
Miss Jones, that's just his personality.

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:19.150
Well, who does he think he is?

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:20.950
Doesn't he know we have a busy station to run?

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:22.910
He thinks he's better than everyone else.

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:23.930
I know.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:25.030
Isn't it great?

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:25.490
Oh.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:27.430
Hey, don't worry about it, Jonesy.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:28.210
You're going to get what you want.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:28.870
Which is what?

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:30.000
To get into the knickers.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.030
The middle air club, that's what!

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.030
Don't you see, I got the whole thing planned out.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.030
Once I'm in, I can get you guys in.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.200
Is that clever or is that clever?

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:44.340
I don't think you understand what's going on here.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.290
Billy, do I look like the type of man

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:49.290
who doesn't know what's going on?

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:58.100
The meeting of the clean club is in session.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:00.000
Thank goodness we got rid of that guy.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.250
I wish we could join your clubs, Mr. Conductor.

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:06.810
"Schemers" doesn't sound like much fun.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:12.060
Well, Stacy says that Mr. Hume thinks he's better than everyone else.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:15.930
But everybody wants to feel good about themselves, don't they?

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:16.750
Yeah.

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:19.710
Well, some people feel too good about themselves.

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:22.640
The engines on the island of Sodor have the same problem.

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:25.850
Take the story of Oliver, the new engine.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:30.000
Well, you can't take it unless I give it to you.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:40.040
On a clear day when the sky is blue and there's just enough breeze to blow the clouds away,

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:49.040
you can stand on the big hill above the valley and watch Duck and Oliver far below busily at work on Duck's branch line near the sea.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:54.000
The two engines are very proud of their matching coats of gleaming color.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.160
Oliver often talks about the time that Douglas saved him from scrap.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:04.220
wasn't for his help, Oliver will say. I might have been caught when I ran away from the

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:10.950
scrapyard, and I would never have come to live here on Sir Topham Hatt's railway. The

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:17.080
other engines all wanted to know about Oliver's adventures. Amazing, remarked Henry. Oliver,

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:23.050
said James, has resource. And sagacity, put in Gordon. What does that mean, whispered

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:30.000
Percy. I think, replied Thomas, it's about being clever and wise. He is, finished Gordon, an

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.640
to us all I'm sorry to say that Oliver became very puffed up in the smoke box

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:42.100
Henry says I'm amazing he's right he whistled as he swooshed along the line

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:52.290
one day Sir Topham Hatt came to see him you are doing well now you must learn how to look after

00:16:52.290 --> 00:17:00.000
freight cars every wise engine knows that you cannot trust freight cars the other engines warned

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:01.890
but he took no notice.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.600
You think I can't manage, he said, huffily.

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:12.270
Gordon knows better. He says I'm sagacious.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:17.050
You may be good gracious, or whatever you call it,

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:20.070
but cars can be troublesome, and...

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:22.110
Say no more, Duck, interrupted Donald.

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:25.680
It's a pity, but the wee engine'll just have to learn for himself.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.730
Oliver pulled some loaded cars to a siding,

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:30.000
and...

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:38.890
and pushed the empties to the chute.

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:44.080
Then he came back to take the loaded cars away.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.080
But they were comfortable and didn't wanna move.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.910
What right is he to poke his funnel in here?

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:54.580
We want Duck! Or Donald! Or Douglas!

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:56.580
Look sharp, puffed Oliver.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:00.000
That's not the way to speak, hissed the cars.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.220
"We'll get even."

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:04.160
Oliver heard nothing.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:06.160
The cars moved smoothly at first.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:09.780
Then suddenly, Oliver felt them push forward.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:14.190
His driver applied the brakes,

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:16.770
but they were useless against the surging cars.

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.450
"On! On! On!" yelled the cars.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:23.330
Oliver fought hard, but still they forced him on and on.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.410
At last, the cars grew tired.

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:37.820
I'm winning, gasped Oliver.

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:39.820
But it was too late.

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:47.880
Oliver lay bruised and bemused,

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:50.200
bunkered down in the turntable well.

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:52.230
Duck surveyed the damage.

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:53.980
Hello, Oliver.

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.270
Are you being a good gracious engine?

00:18:56.780 --> 00:19:00.000
Beg pardon, but we really don't like this sort of surprise.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:06.440
Donald and Douglas will miss their turntable until it is mended.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.620
That evening, Oliver was hauled gently to safety.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:16.000
"I'm sorry, sir," he said to Sir Topham Hatt, "I should have listened to Duck's advice.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.320
I don't feel good gracious, or whatever it is, I just feel silly."

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:26.850
"Well, Oliver," replied Sir Topham Hatt, "now you know the damage cars can do."

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:30.000
"Yes, I do, sir."

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.080
I-I look like a load of scrap iron.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:37.050
"Oh, I don't think so," laughed Sir Topham Hatt.

